      "text": "There are 3! (3 factorial) ways for the first three to be arranged: 3! = 3 × 2 × 1 = 6. \nThen, after the first three are arranged, there is only one way for Jordan to be included in the race: the fourth place.\n\nBy the rule of product, there are a total of $6 \\times 1 = \\boxed{6}$ different orders.\n\nTo determine the number of different orders in which Jordan and his three best friends can run the relay race, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the number of ways to arrange the first three runners:**\n   Jordan is fixed in the fourth position, so we only need to consider the arrangements of his three friends. The number of ways to arrange 3 friends is given by the factorial of 3, denoted as \\(3!\\).\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to arrange the runners:**\n   Since Jordan is fixed in the fourth position, we only need to consider the arrangements of his three friends. There are 6 different ways to arrange these three friends in the first three positions.\n\n3. **Calculate the total number of different orders:**\n   Since the position of Jordan (the fourth runner) is fixed, the remaining five positions (first three runners plus Jordan) can be arranged in \\(3!\\) ways. Therefore, the total number of different orders is:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n   Here, the term \\(1\\) is because there is only one way to place Jordan in the fourth position after his three friends have been arranged in the first three positions.\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
