      "text": "We write out five people are running a race in the following table.\n\n$$\\begin{array}{c||c|c|c|c} Seems Like & Jordan & First Runner & Second Runner & Third Runner \\hline Still Running & \\bullet\\bullet\\bullet & \\bullet & \\bullet & \\bullet & \\bullet \\end{array}$$\n\n\nFor each runner in the race, there are four possible ways that runners can run the event, given the constraints stated above.  For the first runner, since Jordan must run the fourth lap, there are four possible ways for the first runner to run the event.  For the second runner, since Jordan has run, there are three runners left to choose from and Jordan can run the first lap, Jordan or one of the other two runners can run the second lap, or the other one can run the second lap.  Since Jordan can run or not the fourth lap, there are three possible ways the second runner can run the fourth lap.  For the third runner, there are two runners left so two possible ways the third runner can run the fourth lap.  For the remaining runner, that is runner four, there is one way the original four runners can run the fourth lap.  We multiply these values and divide by the number of ways to pair up first and second for identical teams.\n\nThere are $\\frac{4\\times 3\\times 3\\times 2\\times 1}{4!}=\\frac{24}{24}=1$ ways solution.\n\nQuinn is 16. The sum of the ages of Beth and Quinn will be 41. How old is Beth?\n\nWe are given that Quinn is 16 and the sum of the ages of Beth and Quinn will be 41.\n\nLet's represent the age of Beth as $x$. The sum of the ages of Beth and Quinn will be $x + 16$.\n\nWe know that the sum of the ages is 41, so we can set up the equation $x + 16 = 41$.\n\nTo solve for $x$, we can subtract 16 from both sides of the equation:\n\n$x + 16 - 16 = 41 - 16$\n\n$x = 25$.\n\nTherefore, Beth is 25 years old.\n\nQuinn and Beth are both planning to save money for college. If Quinn saves $25 each month for 8 months, and Beth saves $15 each month for 12 months, how much more money will Beth have saved than Quinn?\n\nLet's start by calculating the total amount of money Quinn and Beth will have saved after 8 months, 12 months, and the difference in their savings.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. Calculate Quinn's total savings:\n   - Quinn saves $25 each month.\n   - Quinn saves for 8 months. \n   \\[\n   \\text{Quinn's savings} = 25 \\times 8\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate Beth's total savings:\n   - Beth saves $15 each month.\n   - Beth saves for 12 months. \n   \\[\n   \\text{Beth's savings} = 15 \\times 12\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate the difference between Beth's and Quinn's savings:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Difference} = (\\text{Beth's savings}) - (\\text{Quinn's savings})\n   \\]",
